\subsection{The mock Canvas API instance}

Every command receives a [[canvas]] parameter---an instance of the Canvas API
client.
We mock this as the root of our API object hierarchy.

Unlike the objects below, the client cannot be a real [[Canvas]] object: every
one of its methods is a request, and the tests need to decide what each call
returns ([[mock_canvas.get_courses.return_value = ...]]).
So we stay with a mock, but an \emph{autospecced} one.
[[create_autospec(Canvas, instance=True)]] has exactly the methods of
[[canvasapi.Canvas]], with their signatures: a misspelled method raises
[[AttributeError]] and a call with arguments the real method does not take
raises [[TypeError]], where a plain [[MagicMock]] would accept both.
The values these methods return are plain [[MagicMock]]s unless a test sets
them.
<<mock canvas api fixture>>=
from unittest.mock import create_autospec
from canvasapi import Canvas


@pytest.fixture
def mock_canvas():
    """
    Autospecced Canvas API instance.

    Provides get_courses() method that returns a list of courses.
    Tests can customize the return value to simulate different scenarios.
    """
    canvas = create_autospec(Canvas, instance=True)
    canvas.get_courses.return_value = []
    return canvas
@

\subsection{Mock course objects}

Courses are central to Canvas---almost every operation starts by selecting a
course.
We provide both single-course and multi-course fixtures.

\subsubsection{Design decision: real objects, not mocks}
\label{real-objects-not-mocks}

We face a choice: should a test course be a [[MagicMock]] with the attributes
we set on it, or a real [[canvasapi.course.Course]]?

The two differ in what happens when the code reads an attribute that the
fixture does not set.
A [[MagicMock]] invents it: [[course.term]] is then another [[MagicMock]],
which is truthy, compares unequal to everything and formats as a long
[[<MagicMock ...>]] string.
A test can therefore stay green while the code reads an attribute Canvas never
sends, or one that Canvas renamed.
A real [[Course]] has exactly the attributes in the JSON it was built from, so
the same read raises [[AttributeError]] and the test fails where the user's
run would.

So each fixture builds the real [[canvasapi]] class from the fields Canvas
sends, on a [[MagicMock]] requester.
The requester is only used when a method makes a request, so building and
reading the object stays offline.
\begin{itemize}
\item We include the fields our code reads that Canvas sends for the request
our code makes.
[[canvas.get_courses()]] without [[include]] sends [[id]], [[name]],
[[course_code]], [[start_at]] and [[end_at]] (the dates are [[null]] when
unset), but not [[term]]: Canvas adds that only for [[include[]=term]], which
we never ask for.
Earlier versions of these fixtures set a [[term]], which no code reads; it is
gone, since its only effect was to make the fake look richer than the real
thing.
\item We omit the many fields our code never reads, to keep tests readable.
\item Tests can still add attributes when they need them, since
[[canvasapi]] objects accept new attributes as any Python object does.
\end{itemize}

The methods our code calls, such as [[get_assignments()]] and [[get_users()]],
would make requests.
We replace them on the instance with stubs that return an empty list.
The stubs are autospecced from the real bound method, so a call with
arguments the real method does not accept still fails.
Tests override them by assigning a new stub to the attribute, for example
[[mock_course.get_users = Mock(return_value=[student])]]; a plain [[Mock]]
like that one gives up the signature check, while setting the
[[return_value]] of the fixture's stub keeps it.
<<mock course fixtures>>=
from canvasapi.course import Course


def stub_canvas_method(canvas_object, name, return_value):
    """Replace a request-making method by a stub with its signature."""
    stub = create_autospec(getattr(canvas_object, name),
                           return_value=return_value)
    setattr(canvas_object, name, stub)
    return stub


def make_course(attributes):
    """Build a real Course from Canvas JSON, with its list methods stubbed."""
    course = Course(MagicMock(),
                    {"start_at": None, "end_at": None, **attributes})
    stub_canvas_method(course, "get_assignments", [])
    stub_canvas_method(course, "get_users", [])
    return course


@pytest.fixture
def mock_course():
    """
    Canvas Course object with realistic attributes.

    Attributes:
        id: Course ID (12345)
        name: Course name ("Test Course")
        course_code: Short code ("TEST101")
        start_at, end_at: None, as for a course without dates

    Methods:
        get_assignments(): Returns empty list (override in tests)
        get_users(): Returns empty list (override in tests)
    """
    return make_course({
        "id": 12345,
        "name": "Test Course",
        "course_code": "TEST101",
    })


@pytest.fixture
def mock_courses():
    """
    Returns a list of three courses with different names.

    Useful for testing filtering and selection logic.
    Uses variation theory: three courses with same structure,
    different data (names, codes, IDs).
    """
    return [
        make_course({"id": 1001, "name": "Introduction to Programming",
                     "course_code": "CS101"}),
        make_course({"id": 1002, "name": "Advanced Algorithms",
                     "course_code": "CS201"}),
        make_course({"id": 1003, "name": "Data Structures",
                     "course_code": "CS102"}),
    ]
@

\subsection{Mock assignment objects}

Assignments represent tasks that students submit.
Our assignments include common attributes like due dates, point values,
and submission types.
Both objects also carry a [[course_id]], which [[canvasapi]] uses to build the
URLs of their methods: Canvas sends it with every assignment, and
[[Course.get_assignment_groups]] adds it to each group, whose JSON lacks it.
We use the id of [[mock_course]].
[[Assignment]] parses [[due_at]] into [[due_at_date]] when it is built, as it
does for objects fetched from Canvas.
<<mock assignment fixtures>>=
from canvasapi.assignment import Assignment, AssignmentGroup


@pytest.fixture
def mock_assignment():
    """
    Canvas Assignment object.

    Attributes:
        id: Assignment ID
        course_id: ID of the course (that of mock_course)
        name: Assignment name
        due_at: ISO 8601 due date string (or None)
        points_possible: Maximum points
        published: Whether assignment is published
        assignment_group_id: ID of assignment group
        submission_types: List of allowed submission types

    Methods:
        get_submissions(): Returns empty list (override in tests)
    """
    assignment = Assignment(MagicMock(), {
        "id": 5001,
        "course_id": 12345,
        "name": "Homework 1",
        "due_at": "2023-12-15T23:59:00Z",
        "points_possible": 100.0,
        "published": True,
        "assignment_group_id": 2001,
        "submission_types": ["online_text_entry", "online_upload"],
    })
    stub_canvas_method(assignment, "get_submissions", [])

    return assignment


@pytest.fixture
def mock_assignment_group():
    """
    Canvas AssignmentGroup object.

    Assignment groups categorize assignments (e.g., "Homeworks", "Exams").

    Attributes:
        id: Group ID
        course_id: ID of the course (that of mock_course)
        name: Group name
        position: Sort order
        group_weight: Percentage of final grade
    """
    return AssignmentGroup(MagicMock(), {
        "id": 2001,
        "course_id": 12345,
        "name": "Homeworks",
        "position": 1,
        "group_weight": 40.0,
    })
