\section{Real Canvas objects for the grade summary modules}
\label{grading-objects}

The fixtures above are single objects with fixed values, built from real
[[canvasapi]] classes for the reason given in \cref{real-objects-not-mocks}:
with a [[MagicMock]] any attribute exists, so a test keeps passing when the
code reads an attribute Canvas never sends.
The grade summary modules of \cref{summary-modules} decide students' results
from a handful of attributes---[[grade]], [[submitted_at]], [[graded_at]],
[[quiz_id]], [[submission_types]], a quiz's [[quiz_type]]---so their tests use
real [[canvasapi]] objects instead, built from the same JSON fields that
Canvas sends.
Only the two calls that would reach Canvas are replaced: the assignment's
[[get_submission]] and the course's quiz lookup.

The fixture is a factory, since a test needs several assignments of
different kinds in one group.
It names them after the kinds the summary modules must tell apart: a
[[graded]] assignment (letters or P/F), a [[points]] assignment such as a
FeedbackFruits item graded out of 100, a classic quiz [[survey]] (or, with
[[quiz_type="assignment"]], a graded classic quiz) and a [[new_quiz]].
Passing no submission means that the student has none: [[get_submission]]
raises [[ResourceDoesNotExist]], as Canvas does.

[[canvaslms.grades.surveys]] remembers each quiz's type for the rest of the
process, keyed by quiz id.
A test that reused a quiz id would get the type an earlier test left there,
so an autouse fixture empties that memory before every test; the ids also
come from one counter shared by all tests, so that objects of one test never
look like those of another.
<<grading objects fixture>>=
import itertools
from types import SimpleNamespace

from canvasapi.assignment import Assignment
from canvasapi.exceptions import ResourceDoesNotExist
from canvasapi.quiz import Quiz
from canvasapi.submission import Submission
from canvasapi.user import User

grading_ids = itertools.count(900000)


class QuizCourse:
    """The course side of a quiz lookup, counting the lookups."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.id = 24725
        self.quizzes = {}
        self.lookups = 0

    def get_quiz(self, quiz_id):
        self.lookups += 1
        if quiz_id not in self.quizzes:
            raise ResourceDoesNotExist("Not Found")
        return self.quizzes[quiz_id]

    def get_quizzes(self):
        self.lookups += 1
        return list(self.quizzes.values())


class GradingObjects:
    """Builds real canvasapi objects as the summary modules receive them."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.course = QuizCourse()

    def user(self):
        return User(MagicMock(), {"id": next(grading_ids)})

    def submission(self, grade, submitted_at=None, graded_at=None):
        return Submission(MagicMock(), {
            "id": next(grading_ids),
            "grade": grade,
            "submitted_at": submitted_at,
            "graded_at": graded_at,
            "submission_history": [],
        })

    def assignment(self, attributes, submission=None):
        assignment = Assignment(MagicMock(), {
            "id": next(grading_ids), "course_id": self.course.id,
            **attributes})
        assignment.course = self.course

        def get_submission(user, **kwargs):
            if submission is None:
                raise ResourceDoesNotExist("Not Found")
            return submission

        assignment.get_submission = get_submission
        return assignment

    def graded(self, submission=None, name="Lab 1",
               grading_type="letter_grade"):
        return self.assignment({
            "name": name, "grading_type": grading_type,
            "submission_types": ["online_upload"]}, submission)

    def points(self, submission=None, name="Peer review"):
        return self.assignment({
            "name": name, "grading_type": "points", "points_possible": 100,
            "submission_types": ["external_tool"]}, submission)

    def survey(self, submission=None, name="Course evaluation",
               quiz_type="graded_survey", grading_type="points"):
        quiz_id = next(grading_ids)
        assignment = self.assignment({
            "name": name, "grading_type": grading_type,
            "points_possible": 100,
            "submission_types": ["online_quiz"], "quiz_id": quiz_id,
            "is_quiz_assignment": quiz_type == "assignment"}, submission)
        self.course.quizzes[quiz_id] = Quiz(MagicMock(), {
            "id": quiz_id, "title": name, "quiz_type": quiz_type,
            "assignment_id": assignment.id, "course_id": self.course.id})
        return assignment

    def new_quiz(self, submission=None, name="Topic quiz"):
        return self.assignment({
            "name": name, "grading_type": "points", "points_possible": 100,
            "submission_types": ["external_tool"],
            "is_quiz_assignment": False,
            "is_quiz_lti_assignment": True}, submission)


@pytest.fixture
def grading_objects():
    """Factory for real canvasapi assignments, submissions and quizzes."""
    return GradingObjects()


@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)
def empty_quiz_type_cache():
    """Start every test without remembered quiz types."""
    import canvaslms.grades.surveys
    canvaslms.grades.surveys.quiz_type_cache.clear()
@

\section{Using these fixtures in tests}

With these fixtures in place, writing tests becomes straightforward.
Here's a preview of how they'll be used:

\begin{verbatim}
def test_filter_courses_by_name(mock_canvas, mock_courses):
    """Test that regex filtering works on course names"""
    mock_canvas.get_courses.return_value = mock_courses

    # Test the actual filtering function
    result = filter_courses(mock_canvas, "Programming")

    assert len(result) == 1
    assert result[0].name == "Introduction to Programming"
\end{verbatim}

Notice:
\begin{itemize}
\item We declare which fixtures we need as function parameters
\item Pytest automatically provides them
\item We customize the mock behavior ([[get_courses.return_value]])
\item We test our actual function against realistic mock data
\item The test is clear, focused, and fast
\end{itemize}

\section{Benefits of this approach}

This fixture-based testing strategy provides several advantages:

\begin{description}
\item[Reusability:] Every test can use these fixtures without duplication
\item[Consistency:] All tests use the same real Canvas objects
\item[Maintainability:] If Canvas API changes, we update fixtures in one place
\item[Readability:] Tests focus on behavior, not mock setup
\item[Pedagogical value:] Fixtures serve as documentation of Canvas API
structure
\end{description}

As you read the test examples in subsequent chapters, you'll see these fixtures
in action.
They make tests clear and concise while providing enough realism to catch bugs.

\section{Running the tests}

The [[canvaslms]] project uses Poetry for dependency management.
Test dependencies ([[pytest]], [[pytest-cov]], [[freezegun]], [[pytest-mock]], [[responses]]) are installed in Poetry's virtual environment.

To run tests, you must use [[poetry run pytest]]:

\begin{verbatim}
# Tangle test files from .nw sources
cd tests
make all

# Run all tests with coverage
poetry run pytest -v --cov=canvaslms --cov-report=term-missing

# Or use the make target (which calls poetry run pytest)
make test
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Important:} Simply running [[pytest]] without [[poetry run]] will fail because pytest and its plugins won't be found in the system path.
They exist only in Poetry's virtual environment.

The build system integrates testing:
\begin{itemize}
\item [[make all]] in [[tests/]] tangles test files from .nw sources
\item [[make test]] in [[tests/]] runs [[poetry run pytest]] with coverage
\item [[make all]] in the root directory compiles, builds documentation, and runs all tests
\end{itemize}

\section{Next steps}

In the following chapters, we'll see these fixtures used to test:
\begin{itemize}
\item Time formatting utilities (with [[freezegun]] for deterministic time
tests)
\item Course filtering with regex patterns
\item Assignment and user filtering
\item Submission processing and state management
\item Canvas API extensions (equality, hashing)
\end{itemize}

Each test chapter will show the \emph{expected behavior} through test examples
before diving into the implementation details.
This "tests as examples" approach follows variation theory: we show what the
code \emph{should do} before explaining how it \emph{does} it.
